---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  IslandIntro,
  FloodFillDemo,
  FullScan,
  Comparison,
  Interactive,
} from "../../components/cave/island-count";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Counting Islands"
  description="How to count connected components in a grid using flood fill (BFS/DFS)"
  theme="dark"
>
  <!-- Slide 1: The grid -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        A grid of water and land. Some cells are land, the rest are water. An
        island is a group of land cells connected horizontally or vertically.
        Diagonal doesn't count. How many islands are there?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <IslandIntro client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 2: Flood fill on one island -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        To answer that, we need a way to identify each island. The idea: when we
        find a land cell, we flood-fill outward to mark every cell connected to
        it. That's one island. We use BFS here — start from the cell, visit all
        neighbors, then their neighbors, and so on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once the flood is done, every cell in that island is marked. We won't
        count them again.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <FloodFillDemo client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 3: Full scan -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now we scan the whole grid: left to right, top to bottom. Every time we
        hit an unvisited land cell, we flood-fill it and increment the counter.
        Water and already-visited cells are skipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each island gets a different color. At the end, the counter tells us the
        total number of islands.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <FullScan client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 4: BFS vs DFS -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The flood fill can use either BFS or DFS. Both visit the same cells —
        the difference is the order. BFS expands layer by layer. DFS goes deep
        into one branch before backtracking.
      </p>
      <p>
        For counting islands it doesn't matter which one you use. Both produce
        the same result. The choice is about preference and constraints: DFS is
        simpler to write recursively, BFS avoids deep recursion.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Comparison client:only="react" />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 5: Python code step by step -->
  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's look at the code. We start with the flood fill. It receives the
        grid, a starting position, and the visited set.
      </p>
      <p>We use a queue. We mark the starting cell as visited and add it.</p>
      <p>
        For each cell in the queue, we check its four neighbors: up, down, left,
        right.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a neighbor is inside the grid, is land, and hasn't been visited, we
        mark it and add it to the queue. When the queue empties, the whole
        island is marked.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now the main function. We get the grid dimensions and prepare a visited
        set and a counter.
      </p>
      <p>We scan every cell, row by row, left to right.</p>
      <p>If it's water or already visited, we skip it.</p>
      <p>
        Otherwise, it's a new island. We increment the counter and call flood
        fill to mark all its cells.
      </p>
      <p>When the scan finishes, we return the count.</p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def flood_fill(grid, r, c, visited):
    # STEP: Start BFS from (r, c)
    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])
    queue = [(r, c)]
    visited.add((r, c))
    # STEP: Process queue until island is fully marked
    while queue:
        cr, cc = queue.pop(0)
        for dr, dc in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:
            nr, nc = cr + dr, cc + dc
            # STEP: Add unvisited land neighbors
            if (0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols
                    and grid[nr][nc] == "#"
                    and (nr, nc) not in visited):
                visited.add((nr, nc))
                queue.append((nr, nc))

# STEP: Main function
def count_islands(grid: list[str]) -> int:
    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])
    visited = set()
    count = 0
    # STEP: Scan every cell
    for r in range(rows):
        for c in range(cols):
            # STEP: Skip water and already-visited cells
            if grid[r][c] == "~" or (r, c) in visited:
                continue
            # STEP: New island — count it and flood fill
            count += 1
            flood_fill(grid, r, c, visited)
    # STEP: Return total
    return count`}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 6: Complexity -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Time complexity: O(rows × cols). We visit every cell at most twice —
        once in the scan, once during flood fill.
      </p>
      <p>
        Space complexity: O(rows × cols) for the visited set. The queue or stack
        for the flood fill is bounded by the size of the largest island.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <h2 style="margin-bottom: 0.5em; font-size: 1.6em;">Complexity</h2>
    <table
      style="width: 70%; margin: 0 auto; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 1.1em; text-align: center; zoom: 1.3;"
    >
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="padding: 0.5em 1em; border-bottom: 2px solid #555;"></th>
          <th
            style="padding: 0.5em 1em; border-bottom: 2px solid #555; font-weight: 900;"
            >Value</th
          >
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Time</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em; font-family: monospace;">O(R × C)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Space</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em; font-family: monospace;">O(R × C)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td
            style="padding: 0.55em 1em; text-align: left; opacity: 0.6; font-size: 0.9em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em;"
            >Flood fill</td
          >
          <td style="padding: 0.55em 1em;">BFS or DFS — same result</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 6: Interactive -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Try it yourself. Click cells to toggle land and water, then press "Count
        Islands" to run the scan. Each island gets its own color.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <Interactive client:only="react" />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
